Fixed-Block Memory Pool Implementation

This implementation provides a simple fixed-size memory pool built on top of a single bucket of preallocated memory blocks.
The pool is designed for fast, predictable allocation and deallocation of uniformly sized memory blocks, avoiding heap allocations and minimizing runtime overhead.

Memory is allocated from a contiguous buffer and managed using a lightweight freelist, making this pool suitable for low-latency systems, object pooling, and scenarios where allocation patterns are known in advance.

Properties and Advantages

Fixed-size block allocation
All allocations return memory blocks of the same size (block_size), enabling constant-time allocation and deallocation.

Preallocated contiguous storage
Memory is allocated upfront as a single contiguous buffer, eliminating runtime heap usage and ensuring predictable memory layout.

O(1) allocation and release
Allocation and release operations are simple index manipulations on a freelist, with no searching, locking, or system calls.

Low-latency behavior

No dynamic memory allocation on the hot path

No locks or synchronization primitives

Minimal instruction count per operation

Cache-friendly design

Contiguous memory improves cache locality

Fixed-size blocks reduce fragmentation

Simple array-based freelist

Deterministic memory usage
The pool has a fixed capacity (block_length) and predictable behavior under load. Allocation fails cleanly when capacity is exhausted.

Suitable for object pooling
Can be used to back higher-level abstractions such as:

Object pools

Zero-copy queues

Message passing systems

Trading or networking data structures

Usage Notes and Constraints

This memory pool is not thread-safe. External synchronization is required if used across multiple threads.

All allocated blocks must be returned to the pool using release() to avoid exhaustion.

The pool does not track ownership or validate pointers on release; correct usage is the caller’s responsibility.

Blocks are returned as raw char*; placement new can be used to construct objects in-place if needed.

Intended Use Cases

Low-latency systems requiring predictable allocation behavior

Fixed-size object allocation

Object reuse in performance-critical pipelines

Memory backing for zero-copy queues or message passing systems
